  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=101",
      "html": "<p><strong>Scientific name:</strong> Malapterurus microstoma Poll &amp; Gosse, 1969</p><p><strong>Common names:</strong> Dwarf Electric Catfish, Zitterwels (Germany)</p><p><strong>Size:</strong> 555 mm (21.9\") SL</p><p><strong>Identification:</strong> Easily distinguished by its narrow snout and tooth patches, and horizontally based pectoral fins placed low on the body.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=101",
      "html": "<p>Africa: restricted to the Congo River system; absent from the lowland, central portion of the basin. Type locality: Yangambi, Congo River, Democratic Republic of Congo.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=101",
      "html": "<p>pH: 6.0 - 7.0</p><p>Temperature: 23.0-30.0°C (73.4-86°F)</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=101",
      "html": "<p>A dimly lit tank with a suitably sized retreat - appears to prefer stone over wood.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=101",
      "html": "<p>Feeds readily on all manner of frozen and live food. Will also take meaty food such as frozen meat but will only take prepared foods if nothing else is available.</p><p>Gut contents contained large amounts of detritus along with animal remains, suggesting that this species is a generalist bottom forager rather than a strict piscivore.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=101",
      "html": "<p>Best kept alone, given its electrogenic ability.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=101",
      "html": "<p>Possibly spawns in burrows along river banks and may exhibit brood care. Females are apparently not sexually mature until 40-50 cm SL. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=101",
      "html": "<p>IUCN Red List category: Least Concern (last assessed 2009).</p><p>Etymology: Malapterurus: from the Greek malakos, meaning soft, pteron, meaning fin and oura, meaning tail; presumably in reference to the adipose fin, which appears all the more prominent in the absence of the rayed dorsal fin. Microstoma: from the Greek mikros, meaning small and stoma, meaning mouth (or opening); in reference to the small mouth of this species.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=101",
      "html": "<p>Bulletin de l'Institut Royal des Sciences Naturelles de Belgique v. 45 (no. 38), pp 8, Pls. 1, 2 (fig. b), 3 (fig. b).</p>"
    }
  ],
